  Deferred (each item names how to finish it):
    1. Vault path — the obsidian MCP entry shell is written but the vault path is empty; open Claude Code and run /aide — the orchestrator will prompt for it
    2. Brain vault scaffolding (directories, playbook hub, vault CLAUDE.md) — follows the vault path above (same /aide run)
    3. IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>

============================================================
```

The existing `github` MCP server entry survives untouched. The five pre-existing files are explicitly named in the warning so the user can investigate whether their on-disk versions are current.

A developer runs the installer a second time on a project where every canonical artifact is already present:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[exists] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub already present
[exists] .aide/docs/index.md — already present
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
...
[exists] .mcp.json — aide server already configured
[exists] .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs — aide-tree launcher already present

Already set up.
```

No files were modified and no categories remained deferred in this run (brain vault, obsidian MCP path, and IDE were all resolved in a prior run), so the warning block is suppressed and the plain completion line is printed instead. Exit code 0. This is the idempotency contract the frontmatter requires.

A developer runs the installer against a project whose `.mcp.json` has a trailing comma from a hand-edit mistake:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
Error: .mcp.json exists but contains invalid JSON. Fix the syntax error and re-run.
```

Exit code 1. No files were written. Not even the methodology stub, the doc hub, the commands, the agents, or the launcher — the CLI aborts the whole run because continuing would write into a project whose MCP wiring is already broken. The user fixes the syntax and re-runs; the second run installs cleanly.

## Bad examples

The CLI silently replaces the user's existing `.mcp.json`:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry
...
Done. Open Claude Code and run /aide:upgrade to reconcile any existing files.
```

The user had `github`, `obsidian`, and two custom MCP servers configured. All four are gone, overwritten by a fresh file containing only the `aide` entry. The CLI decided the on-disk config was stale and assumed it could replace it — the exact failure mode the frontmatter's "never overwrites" contract exists to prevent. The user has no way of knowing until their other MCP servers stop appearing in Claude Code.

The CLI finishes with files skipped but never surfaces the skip list:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md — pipeline agent
[exists] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — already present
...
Done.
```

Three files were skipped across methodology, agents, and commands. The user sees `[exists]` markers in the stream but no summary — scrolling back through 35 lines to reconstruct the skip list is the kind of work the warning block is supposed to do for them. They walk away thinking the install is complete, then trip on an outdated agent file a week later and have no record of which files the CLI did not write.

The CLI surfaces skips inline with the log instead of as a loud block:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .aide/docs/aide-template.md — canonical AIDE template
[exists] .aide/docs/aide-spec.md — already present
WARNING: skipped because file exists
[created] .claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md — pipeline agent
[exists] .claude/agents/aide/aide-spec-writer.md — already present
WARNING: skipped because file exists
...
Done. Open Claude Code and run /aide:upgrade to reconcile skipped files.
```

The warnings are structurally inline with the `[created]`/`[exists]` lines, using the same font weight, the same indentation, and the same width as the rest of the log. A user skimming the output reads every line as routine status and never registers that the install is incomplete. This is the "quiet warning" failure mode the undesired outcomes explicitly name — the warning has to break the log's visual rhythm with borders and uppercase heading to register.

The CLI aborts the entire run on a single file's write failure:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
[created] .aide/docs/index.md — methodology hub index
Error: EACCES: permission denied, open '.aide/docs/aide-spec.md'
```

Exit code 1. The methodology stub made it in, nothing else did — not the rest of the doc hub, not the slash commands, not the agents, not the MCP entry, not the launcher. The user has to chase down the permission issue and re-run before they can open Claude Code and start using `/aide`. A single per-file failure must roll into the warning alongside skipped files and let the rest of the install continue.

The CLI attempts to guess a brain vault path:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] .mcp.json — aide and obsidian MCP server entries
Detected brain vault at ../my-brain — wired obsidian MCP server
Done.
```

The user's sibling `../my-brain` directory is actually their spouse's personal journal vault, not an AIDE brain. The CLI discovered it from a filesystem scan, wired the `obsidian` entry to point at it, and silently committed the user's pipeline to writing research notes into a vault they never consented to. Brain vault is an inherent decision — the CLI has no business resolving it, and the frontmatter explicitly forbids guessing from a sibling folder, env var, or platform default.

The CLI prompts the user mid-run:

```
$ npx @aidemd-mcp/server init
[created] CLAUDE.md — AIDE methodology pointer stub
The .aide/docs/ hub will contain 9 files. Continue? [y/N]
```

The happy path must be zero-interaction. The `npx` invocation is the opt-in; a confirmation prompt adds friction to a command whose whole purpose is to be runnable unattended, and it violates the frontmatter contract that the CLI cannot prompt because there is no UI for it.

## References

- `src/tools/init/.aide` (sister spec) -- confirms the shared overarching semantic: prompts are reserved for overwrites and inherent decisions, silent-on-create is the fast-path rule. The CLI adaptation is skip-or-abort because terminal context has no UI to prompt with — the content of this spec's "skip-on-exists as only safe branch" strategy paragraph.
- `src/.aide` -- parent spec; source of the "two delivery channels" frame and the idempotency-as-invariant contract that carries down into the CLI's repeated-run behavior and the `Already set up.` suppression rule.
- `src/cli/init/index.ts` -- current `runInit` shape: sequential per-artifact `await`, status-prefixed log lines, final completion line. The strategy extends this shape to the full canonical-artifact set without changing the log format.
- `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts` -- reference implementation of read-parse-merge-write with dual-key check and abort-on-parse-failure (lines 35-44 for the parse/throw, line 47 for the dual-key match). The strategy reuses this helper as-is for the MCP merge.
- `src/cli/init/writeInitCommand/index.ts` and `src/cli/init/writeAideTree/index.ts` -- reference implementations of the stat-then-write-or-skip pattern with parent-directory creation and canonical-content resolution via `readCanonicalDoc`. The strategy models every new per-artifact helper on this shape.
- `src/tools/init/wireMcp/index.ts` -- `mcpEntry()` (lines 20-25) returns the platform-branching server entry the CLI writes verbatim; the dual-key check at line 47 is the contract `writeMcpEntry` matches so subsequent `aide_init` MCP steps report `exists` instead of adding a duplicate.
- `src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts` -- `readCanonicalDoc` (line 161) is the content-resolution primitive; `DOC_PATHS` (lines 34-69) is the canonical-name-to-path registry; `listMethodologyDocs`/`listAgents`/`listSkills` (lines 202-220) are the canonical enumerations the CLI iterates to install each category. The strategy reuses these directly, bypassing the planner helpers built on top of them.
- `src/tools/init/installMethodologyDocs/index.ts`, `src/tools/init/installAgents/index.ts`, `src/tools/init/installSkills/index.ts`, `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/index.ts` -- planner helpers that return `InitStep[]` for agent-driven application. The strategy explicitly does NOT reuse these (they are planners for `applySteps`) and instead mirrors their enumeration pattern in direct imperative writes.
- `src/tools/init/writeMethodology/index.ts` -- source of `composeStub` (line 61) and the `METHODOLOGY_MARKER` (line 12) constant via `getMethodologyMarker`; the CLI uses both to compose the pointer stub and detect the marker for skip-on-exists on the host config file.
- `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/index.ts` -- `COMMANDS` registry (lines 28-46) enumerates the 13 pipeline slash commands the CLI installs; the `hostPath` field defines the `.claude/commands/aide/<phase>.md` layout.
- `.aide/docs/aide-template.md` -- canonical body-section contract for the spec itself: Context explains why the module exists, Strategy distills decisions with inline citations, Good/Bad examples are real output, References logs what was actually used during synthesis.
- `.aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md` and `.aide/docs/aide-spec.md` -- methodology docs that establish the "read specs before code" and "stub-in-config, hub-on-disk" patterns the pointer-stub + doc-hub install enacts at the delivery layer.
